  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/e4335622f7505350155fd5c84ce1cbdbb6c4fba4570b8cf4a4cd8982403ffede.png)\n\nAn aquarium has $45$ fish. The ratio of goldfish to guppies is shown in the diagram above. How many guppies are in the aquarium?",
  "worked_solution": "The tape diagram shows $2$ boxes corresponding to goldfish, and $3$ boxes corresponding to guppies. Therefore, the ratio of goldfish to guppies is $2:3.$\n\nWe are told that there are $45$ fish in total. The tape diagram has $2+3=5$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $45÷5=9$ fish.\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/069481a1ab6b18ebe6bf20053b793c49b54c06352f3acfc218aae32e34cd8f50.png)\n\nSince there are $3$ boxes corresponding to guppies, and each box represents $9$ fish, the number of guppies must be:\n\n$$\n3\\times 9=27.\n$$\n\nSo, there are $27$ guppies in the aquarium.",
